(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Physics I (Mechanics & Waves)"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Physics I (Mechanics & Waves)"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Physics I (Mechanics and Waves): Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nUse g = 9.8 m/s^2 and the speed of sound 343 m/s unless told otherwise. For every problem draw a diagram, state your axes, write the law you use, solve symbolically, then substitute numbers and check units. Marks are for method as well as the final answer.\n\n# Assignment 1: Kinematics and Newton's Laws (60 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. (8 marks)** (a) A car moving at 30 m/s brakes uniformly at 6 m/s^2. Find the stopping time and distance [3]. (b) Car A moves at constant 20 m/s. Car B starts from rest at the same point 5 s after A passes, accelerating at 4 m/s^2. When and where does B catch A, and how fast is B moving then? [5]\n\n**Q2. (7 marks)** A ball is thrown vertically upward at 15 m/s from the edge of a 30 m cliff. (a) How long until it lands at the bottom? [4] (b) What is its speed on impact? [2] (c) What maximum height above the cliff top does it reach? [1]\n\n**Q3. (8 marks)** A projectile is launched from level ground at 25 m/s at 40° above horizontal. Find (a) the time of flight, (b) the maximum height, (c) the range [4 total]. (d) At what two launch angles would the range be 50 m? [4]\n\n**Q4. (7 marks)** (a) What is the greatest speed at which a car can round a flat curve of radius 50 m if μ_s = 0.6? [3] (b) At what angle should a frictionless curve of radius 50 m be banked for a design speed of 20 m/s? [4]\n\n**Q5. (8 marks)** (a) An Atwood machine has masses 3 kg and 5 kg. Find the acceleration and the tension [4]. (b) A block slides down a 30° incline with μ_k = 0.2. Find its acceleration and the time to slide 4 m from rest [4].\n\n**Q6. (8 marks)** A 4 kg block rests on a table (μ_k = 0.25) and is connected by a rope over a frictionless pulley to a 2 kg hanging mass. Find the acceleration and the rope tension. Also check whether the system starts moving if μ_s = 0.4.\n\n**Q7. (7 marks)** A river 200 m wide flows at 3 m/s. A boat has speed 5 m/s relative to the water. (a) Find the minimum crossing time and the downstream drift [3]. (b) At what angle must it be aimed to land directly opposite, and how long does that take? [4]\n\n**Q8. (7 marks)** A 70 kg person stands on a scale in an elevator. Find the scale reading when the elevator (a) accelerates upward at 2 m/s^2, (b) accelerates downward at 2 m/s^2, (c) is in free fall, (d) moves at constant velocity. Sketch the free-body diagram for (a).\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: t = v0/a = 30/6 = **5 s**.\nStep 2: d = v0²/(2a) = 900/12 = **75 m**. (1 mark each for time and distance, 1 for correct formula.)\n(b) Step 1: take t = 0 when A passes the origin: x_A = 20 t. For t ≥ 5: x_B = (1/2)(4)(t - 5)² = 2(t - 5)².\nStep 2: set equal: 20 t = 2(t² - 10 t + 25), so t² - 20 t + 25 = 0.\nStep 3: t = 10 ± sqrt 75 = 18.66 s or 1.34 s. Only t > 5 is valid: **t = 18.7 s**.\nStep 4: position x = 20 × 18.66 = **373 m**.\nStep 5: B's speed v = 4(18.66 - 5) = **54.6 m/s**. (1 mark setup, 1 equations, 1 quadratic, 1 root selection, 1 speed.)\n\n**Q2.** Take up as positive, origin at the cliff top: y = 15 t - 4.9 t^2, landing at y = -30.\n(a) Step 1: 4.9 t² - 15 t - 30 = 0.\nStep 2: t = (15 + sqrt(225 + 4 × 4.9 × 30))/9.8 = (15 + sqrt(813))/9.8 = (15 + 28.51)/9.8 = **4.44 s** (discard the negative root).\n(b) v = 15 - 9.8(4.44) = -28.5 m/s, so the speed is **28.5 m/s** (check: sqrt(15^2 + 2 · 9.8 · 30) = 28.5 ✓).\n(c) H = v0^2/(2g) = 225/19.6 = **11.5 m**.\n\n**Q3.** Step 0: v0 sin θ = 25 sin 40° = 16.07 m/s, v0 cos θ = 25 cos 40° = 19.15 m/s.\n(a) T = 2 v0 sin θ/g = 2(16.07)/9.8 = **3.28 s**.\n(b) H = (v0 sin θ)²/(2g) = 16.07²/19.6 = **13.2 m**.\n(c) R = v0 cos θ × T = 19.15 × 3.28 = **62.8 m** (check: v0² sin 80°/g = 625 × 0.9848/9.8 = 62.8 ✓).\n(a) T = 2(16.07)/9.8 = **3.28 s**.\n(b) H = 16.07^2/19.6 = **13.2 m**.\n(c) R = 19.15 · 3.28 = **62.8 m** (check: v0^2 sin 80°/g = 625 · 0.9848/9.8 = 62.8 ✓).\n(d) Step 1: sin 2θ = R g/v0² = 50 × 9.8/625 = 0.784.\nStep 2: 2θ = 51.6° or 128.4°, giving **θ = 25.8° or 64.2°**.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1: friction supplies the centripetal force: μ_s m g = m v²/r.\nStep 2: v = sqrt(μ_s g r) = sqrt(0.6 × 9.8 × 50) = sqrt 294 = **17.1 m/s (61.7 km/h)**.\n(b) Step 1: N cos θ = m g and N sin θ = m v²/r.\nStep 2: divide: tan θ = v²/(r g) = 400/490 = 0.816.\nStep 3: **θ = 39.2°**.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Step 1: a = (m2 - m1) g/(m1 + m2) = 2 × 9.8/8 = **2.45 m/s²**.\nStep 2: T = m1 (g + a) = 3 × 12.25 = **36.75 N** (check with m2: 5(9.8 - 2.45) = 36.75 ✓).\n(b) Step 1: along the slope: m a = m g sin θ - μ_k m g cos θ, so a = 9.8 (0.5 - 0.2 × 0.866) = **3.20 m/s²**.\nStep 2: from d = (1/2) a t²: t = sqrt(2 × 4/3.20) = **1.58 s**.\n\n**Q6.** Step 1: check static friction first: the hanging weight is 2 × 9.8 = 19.6 N, while the maximum static friction is 0.4 × 4 × 9.8 = 15.7 N. Since 19.6 N > 15.7 N, the system **does start to move**.\nStep 2: once sliding, f_k = 0.25 × 4 × 9.8 = 9.8 N.\nStep 3: for the hanging mass: m2 g - T = m2 a; for the block: T - f_k = m1 a.\nStep 4: adding: a = (19.6 - 9.8)/6 = **1.63 m/s²**.\nStep 5: T = m2 (g - a) = 2(9.8 - 1.633) = **16.3 N**. (2 marks free-body diagrams, 2 equations, 2 solution, 2 static check.)\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) Aim straight across: t = 200/5 = **40 s**. Drift = 3 · 40 = **120 m**.\n(b) Step 1: the upstream component of the boat's velocity must cancel the current: 5 sin φ = 3, φ = **36.9° upstream of the perpendicular**.\nStep 2: crossing speed = 5 cos φ = 4 m/s, so t = 200/4 = **50 s**.\n\n**Q8.** N - m g = m a (up positive).\n(a) N = 70(9.8 + 2) = **826 N**.\n(b) N = 70(9.8 - 2) = **546 N**.\n(c) a = -g, so N = **0** (apparent weightlessness).\n(d) N = m g = **686 N**.\nFree-body diagram for (a): N upward (longer arrow), m g downward, net force upward.\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 2: Energy, Momentum, Rotation and Gravitation (80 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. (8 marks)** A 2 kg block slides from rest down a frictionless curved ramp of height 1.5 m onto a rough horizontal floor with μ_k = 0.3. Find the speed at the bottom and how far it slides before stopping.\n\n**Q2. (8 marks)** (a) A spring (k = 200 N/m) is compressed 0.10 m and launches a 0.5 kg block along a frictionless table. Find the launch speed [3]. (b) A roller-coaster car starts from rest at height h above the bottom of a frictionless loop of radius R = 4 m. What is the minimum h to complete the loop? [5]\n\n**Q3. (6 marks)** (a) A motor lifts a 500 kg load at constant speed 0.5 m/s. Find the power [2]. (b) A 1200 kg car accelerates from rest to 27 m/s in 8 s. Find the average power delivered to the car's kinetic energy [4].\n\n**Q4. (8 marks)** A 2 kg cart moving at 6 m/s hits a stationary 4 kg cart. Find the final velocities and the kinetic energy lost if the collision is (a) perfectly elastic and (b) perfectly inelastic.\n\n**Q5. (8 marks)** A 10 g bullet is fired into a 2 kg wooden block suspended as a ballistic pendulum. The block (with the bullet) swings up 0.20 m. Find the bullet's speed and the fraction of kinetic energy lost.\n\n**Q6. (8 marks)** A 0.5 kg ball moving at 4 m/s strikes an identical stationary ball in an elastic glancing collision. The first ball is deflected 30° from its original direction. Find both final velocities (speed and direction) and show that the angle between them is 90°.\n\n**Q7. (12 marks)** (a) A solid cylinder (M = 10 kg, R = 0.20 m) rolls without slipping down an incline from a height of 1.2 m. Find its speed at the bottom and compare with a frictionless sliding block [5]. (b) Find the moment of inertia of the cylinder, and the angular acceleration produced by a constant torque of 5 N m [3]. (c) Find the acceleration of the cylinder down a 30° incline [4].\n\n**Q8. (6 marks)** A skater spins at 2.0 rad/s with moment of inertia 4.0 kg m^2. She pulls in her arms so that I = 1.5 kg m^2. Find the new angular speed, the change in kinetic energy, and explain where the energy comes from.\n\n**Q9. (10 marks)** Using G M_E = 3.986 × 10^14 m^3/s^2 and R_E = 6.371 × 10^6 m: (a) find the escape speed from Earth's surface [3]; (b) find the speed and period of a satellite in a circular orbit 400 km above the surface [5]; (c) find the orbital total energy per unit mass at that altitude [2].\n\n**Q10. (6 marks)** A uniform 6.0 m beam of mass 20 kg rests on two supports at its ends. A 60 kg person stands 2.0 m from the left end. Find the support forces.\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.** Step 1: energy conservation on the ramp: m g h = (1/2) m v², so v = sqrt(2 g h) = sqrt(29.4) = **5.42 m/s**.\nStep 2: on the floor, friction does work -μ m g d, so (1/2) m v² = μ m g d and d = v²/(2 μ g) = h/μ = 1.5/0.3 = **5.0 m**. (3 marks speed, 3 marks distance, 2 marks method statement.)\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) (1/2) k x^2 = (1/2) m v^2, so v = x sqrt(k/m) = 0.10 sqrt(400) = **2.0 m/s**.\n(b) Step 1: at the top of the loop the minimum condition is N = 0, so m g = m v_top²/R and v_top² = g R.\nStep 2: energy from the start: m g h = m g (2R) + (1/2) m v_top² = m g (2R) + (1/2) m g R = 2.5 m g R.\nStep 3: **h = 2.5 R = 10 m**.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) P = F v = m g v = 500 · 9.8 · 0.5 = **2450 W**.\n(b) Step 1: ΔK = (1/2)(1200)(27)² = 437,400 J.\nStep 2: P_avg = 437,400/8 = **54.7 kW** (about 73 hp).\n\n**Q4.** Initial momentum 2 · 6 = 12 kg m/s, initial KE = (1/2)(2)(36) = 36 J.\n(a) Elastic: v1' = ((m1 - m2)/(m1 + m2)) v1 = ((2 - 4)/6)(6) = **-2 m/s**; v2' = (2 m1/(m1 + m2)) v1 = (4/6)(6) = **+4 m/s**. Check: momentum 2(-2) + 4(4) = 12 ✓, KE = 4 + 32 = 36 ✓. **Energy lost: 0.**\n(b) Step 1: inelastic: v' = 12/6 = **2 m/s** for both.\nStep 2: KE after = (1/2)(6)(4) = 12 J, so **energy lost 36 - 12 = 24 J (67%)**.\n\n**Q5.** Step 1: after the collision, energy conservation gives v' = sqrt(2 g h) = sqrt(3.92) = 1.98 m/s.\nStep 2: momentum in the collision: m v = (m + M) v', so v = (2.01/0.010)(1.98) = **398 m/s**.\nStep 3: initial KE = (1/2)(0.010)(398)² = 792 J; KE just after = (1/2)(2.01)(1.98)² = 3.94 J.\nStep 4: fraction lost = 1 - 3.94/792 = **99.5%**. (2 marks each for the two stages, 2 for the bullet speed, 2 for the fraction.)\n\n**Q6.** Step 1: For equal masses, an elastic collision with one at rest gives perpendicular outgoing velocities.\nStep 2: Ball 1: v1' = 4 cos 30° = **3.46 m/s at 30° from the original direction**.\nStep 3: Ball 2: v2' = 4 sin 30° = **2.0 m/s at 60° on the other side** (-60°).\nStep 4: Verification: momentum along the original axis: 3.46 cos 30° + 2 cos 60° = 3.0 + 1.0 = 4.0 ✓; transverse: 3.46 sin 30° - 2 sin 60° = 1.73 - 1.73 = 0 ✓; energy: 3.46^2 + 2^2 = 12 + 4 = 16 = 4^2 ✓. The angle between the two velocities is 30° + 60° = 90°. ✓\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) Step 1: I = (1/2) M R², so energy conservation: M g h = (1/2) M v² + (1/2) I ω² = (1/2) M v² + (1/4) M v² = (3/4) M v².\nStep 2: v = sqrt(4 g h/3) = sqrt(4 × 9.8 × 1.2/3) = sqrt(15.68) = **3.96 m/s**.\nStep 3: a sliding block reaches sqrt(2 g h) = 4.85 m/s; the cylinder is slower because a third of the energy goes into rotation.\n(b) I = (1/2)(10)(0.04) = **0.20 kg m^2**. α = τ/I = **25 rad/s^2**.\n(c) a = g sin θ/(1 + 1/2) = (2/3) g sin 30° = **3.27 m/s^2**.\n\n**Q8.** Step 1: L conserved: ω' = I ω/I' = 4(2)/1.5 = **5.33 rad/s**.\nStep 2: K_i = (1/2)(4)(4) = 8 J; K_f = (1/2)(1.5)(5.33)² = 21.3 J. **ΔK = +13.3 J.**\nStep 3: the energy comes from the work her muscles do pulling the arms inward against the (apparent) centrifugal tendency; angular momentum is conserved but kinetic energy is not.\n\n**Q9.**\n(a) v_esc = sqrt(2 G M/R) = sqrt(2 × 3.986 × 10^14/6.371 × 10^6) = sqrt(1.251 × 10^8) = **11.19 km/s**.\n(b) Step 1: r = 6.371 × 10^6 + 4.00 × 10^5 = 6.771 × 10^6 m.\nStep 2: v = sqrt(G M/r) = sqrt(5.887 × 10^7) = **7.67 km/s**.\nStep 3: T = 2π r/v = 2π × 6.771 × 10^6/7672 = 5545 s = **92.4 minutes**.\n(c) E/m = -G M/(2 r) = -3.986 × 10^14/(1.354 × 10^7) = **-2.94 × 10^7 J/kg**.\n\n**Q10.** Step 1: take torques about the left support. The beam's weight (20 × 9.8 = 196 N) acts at 3.0 m, the person (60 × 9.8 = 588 N) at 2.0 m, and the right support force N_R at 6.0 m.\nStep 2: 6 N_R = 196(3) + 588(2) = 588 + 1176 = 1764, so **N_R = 294 N**.\nStep 3: vertical balance: N_L = 784 - 294 = **490 N**.\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 3: Oscillations and Waves (80 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. (10 marks)** A 0.40 kg mass on a spring (k = 40 N/m) oscillates with amplitude 0.050 m. Find (a) ω, T, f [3]; (b) maximum speed and maximum acceleration [2]; (c) total energy [1]; (d) the speed at x = 0.030 m [2]; (e) x(t) if at t = 0 the mass is at x = 0.030 m moving in the +x direction at 0.40 m/s [2].\n\n**Q2. (6 marks)** (a) Find the period of a 1.00 m simple pendulum [2]. (b) What length gives a period of exactly 2 s? [2] (c) What is the period of that pendulum on the Moon (g = 1.62 m/s^2)? [2]\n\n**Q3. (10 marks)** A 1 kg mass on a spring with k = 100 N/m is damped with b = 2 kg/s. Find (a) ω0 and γ = b/(2m) [2]; (b) the damped angular frequency [2]; (c) the time for the amplitude to halve [2]; (d) the quality factor Q [2]; (e) the damping coefficient b that gives critical damping [2].\n\n**Q4. (6 marks)** An oscillator with ω0 = 5 rad/s, m = 1 kg and γ = 0.1 s^-1 is driven by F0 cos(ω t) with F0 = 2 N. Find the steady-state amplitude at resonance and at ω = 2 rad/s.\n\n**Q5. (12 marks)** A string wave is described by y = 0.020 sin(4π x - 100π t) (SI units). Find (a) the amplitude, wavelength, frequency, period, speed [5]; (b) the maximum transverse speed of a string element [2]; (c) the tension if μ = 0.010 kg/m [2]; (d) the average power transmitted [3].\n\n**Q6. (10 marks)** (a) A 0.80 m string fixed at both ends supports waves at v = 200 m/s. Find the first three harmonic frequencies and wavelengths [4]. (b) Find the fundamental of a 0.50 m pipe open at both ends and of the same pipe closed at one end (v = 343 m/s) [3]. (c) Which harmonics are present in each? [3]\n\n**Q7. (10 marks)** (a) A sound has intensity 1.0 × 10^-6 W/m^2. Find its level in dB [2]. (b) A 100 W point source radiates uniformly; find the intensity and level at 10 m, and at 20 m [4]. (c) Two incoherent 60 dB sources sound together; find the total level [2]. (d) How many identical machines of 85 dB each produce 95 dB? [2]\n\n**Q8. (10 marks)** (a) An ambulance siren (700 Hz) moves at 30 m/s toward a stationary observer, then away; find both frequencies [4]. (b) The observer now moves at 20 m/s toward the stationary siren; find the frequency [2]. (c) Two tuning forks, 440 Hz and 444 Hz, sound together; find the beat frequency and period [2]. (d) A jet moves at Mach 2; find the Mach cone half-angle [2].\n\n**Q9. (6 marks)** Two in-phase loudspeakers emit 200 Hz tones. A listener is 3.00 m from one and 3.86 m from the other. Is the interference constructive or destructive? Find the frequencies near 200 Hz that would give exact destructive interference at that point.\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) ω = sqrt(k/m) = sqrt(100) = **10 rad/s**, T = 2π/ω = **0.628 s**, f = **1.59 Hz**.\n(b) v_max = A ω = 0.050 · 10 = **0.50 m/s**; a_max = A ω^2 = **5.0 m/s^2**.\n(c) E = (1/2) k A^2 = 0.5 · 40 · 0.0025 = **0.050 J**.\n(d) v = ω sqrt(A² - x²) = 10 sqrt(0.0025 - 0.0009) = 10 × 0.040 = **0.40 m/s**.\n(e) Step 1: x = A cos(ω t + φ) with v = -A ω sin(ω t + φ).\nStep 2: At t = 0: A cos φ = 0.030 and -A ω sin φ = 0.40, so sin φ = -0.8, cos φ = 0.6, φ = **-0.927 rad (-53.1°)** and A = sqrt(0.03^2 + 0.04^2) = 0.050 m.\nStep 3: So **x(t) = 0.050 cos(10 t - 0.927) m**.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) T = 2π sqrt(L/g) = 2π sqrt(1/9.8) = **2.007 s**.\n(b) L = g T^2/(4π^2) = 9.8 · 4/39.48 = **0.993 m**.\n(c) T = 2π sqrt(0.993/1.62) = 2π(0.783) = **4.92 s** (the Moon's low gravity makes the pendulum slower).\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) ω0 = sqrt(k/m) = **10 rad/s**; γ = 2/(2 · 1) = **1.0 s^-1**.\n(b) ω' = sqrt(ω0^2 - γ^2) = sqrt(99) = **9.95 rad/s**.\n(c) The amplitude decays as e^(-γ t). Halving: e^(-γ t) = 1/2, t = ln 2/γ = **0.693 s**.\n(d) Q = ω0/(2γ) = 10/2 = **5**.\n(e) Critical damping: γ = ω0, so b = 2 m ω0 = **20 kg/s**.\n\n**Q4.** A(ω) = (F0/m)/sqrt((ω0² - ω²)² + (2 γ ω)²).\nStep 1: at resonance ω = ω0 = 5: A = 2/sqrt(0 + (2 × 0.1 × 5)²) = 2/1 = **2.0 m**.\nStep 2: at ω = 2: ω0² - ω² = 21, 2γω = 0.4; A = 2/sqrt(441 + 0.16) = 2/21.0 = **0.0952 m**.\nStep 3: the response at resonance is about 21 times larger.\n\n**Q5.** Compare with y = A sin(k x - ω t): A = 0.020 m, k = 4π rad/m, ω = 100π rad/s.\n(a) **A = 0.020 m, λ = 2π/k = 0.50 m, f = ω/2π = 50 Hz, T = 0.020 s, v = f λ = 25 m/s.** (The wave moves in the +x direction.)\n(b) v_max = A ω = 0.020 · 314.2 = **6.28 m/s**.\n(c) v = sqrt(T/μ), so T = μ v^2 = 0.010 · 625 = **6.25 N**.\n(d) P = (1/2) μ ω² A² v = 0.5 × 0.010 × (314.2)² × (0.020)² × 25: (314.2)² = 98,722; × 0.0004 = 39.49; × 0.005 = 0.1974; × 25 = **4.93 W**.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) f_n = n v/(2L) = n · 200/1.6 = 125 n Hz. **125, 250, 375 Hz** with wavelengths λ_n = 2L/n = **1.6, 0.8, 0.533 m**.\n(b) Open: f1 = v/(2L) = 343/1.0 = **343 Hz**. Closed: f1 = v/(4L) = 343/2.0 = **171.5 Hz**.\n(c) The open pipe has **all harmonics** (343, 686, 1029, ... Hz). The closed pipe has **only odd harmonics** (171.5, 514.5, 857.5, ... Hz).\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) β = 10 log10(10^-6/10^-12) = 10(6) = **60 dB**.\n(b) Step 1: at 10 m: I = P/(4π r²) = 100/(4π × 100) = **0.0796 W/m²**.\nStep 2: β = 10 log10(0.0796/10^-12) = 10(10.90) = **109 dB**.\nStep 3: at 20 m: I = 0.0199 W/m², β = **103 dB** (6 dB lower, the inverse-square law).\n(c) Intensities add: 2 × 10^-6 W/m^2 gives β = 60 + 10 log 2 = **63 dB**.\n(d) A 10 dB increase means ten times the intensity, so **10 machines**.\n\n**Q8.** Use v = 343 m/s.\n(a) Step 1: approaching: f' = f v/(v - v_s) = 700 × 343/(343 - 30) = 700 × 343/313 = **767 Hz**.\nStep 2: receding: f' = 700 × 343/(343 + 30) = 700 × 343/373 = **644 Hz**.\n(b) Moving observer: f' = f (v + v_o)/v = 700 × 363/343 = **741 Hz**.\n(c) f_beat = |444 - 440| = **4 Hz**, a beat period of **0.25 s**.\n(d) sin θ = 1/Mach = 0.5, so **θ = 30°**.\n\n**Q9.** Path difference Δ = 3.86 - 3.00 = 0.86 m. λ = v/f = 343/200 = 1.715 m, so Δ/λ = 0.501, essentially one half wavelength: **destructive interference** (the tone is very faint). Exact destructive interference needs Δ = (n + 1/2) λ = (n + 1/2) v/f, so f = (n + 1/2) v/Δ = (n + 1/2)(343/0.86) = 199.4 Hz, 598 Hz, 997 Hz, ... for n = 0, 1, 2 (the first is the one near 200 Hz).\n","exam":"# Physics I (Mechanics and Waves): Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Use g = 9.8 m/s^2, v_sound = 343 m/s, G M_E = 3.986 × 10^14 m^3/s^2, R_E = 6.371 × 10^6 m. Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** A particle's position is x = 3t^2 - 2t (SI). Its velocity at t = 2 s is\nA) 4 m/s  B) 8 m/s  C) 10 m/s  D) 12 m/s\n\n**2.** An object falls from rest for 4.0 s (no air resistance). The distance fallen is about\nA) 39 m  B) 78 m  C) 157 m  D) 39.2 m/s\n\n**3.** At the highest point of a projectile's path (no air resistance), the velocity is\nA) zero  B) purely horizontal  C) purely vertical  D) at 45°\n\n**4.** A car moves at 10 m/s around a curve of radius 5 m. Its centripetal acceleration is\nA) 2 m/s^2  B) 10 m/s^2  C) 20 m/s^2  D) 50 m/s^2\n\n**5.** A 10 kg block on a floor (μ_k = 0.30) is pushed at constant speed. The horizontal force needed is\nA) 3 N  B) 29.4 N  C) 98 N  D) 0\n\n**6.** The work done by gravity on a 2 kg object that falls 5 m is\nA) -98 J  B) 10 J  C) 98 J  D) 196 J\n\n**7.** A machine does 100 J of work in 4 s. Its power is\nA) 4 W  B) 25 W  C) 100 W  D) 400 W\n\n**8.** A 4 kg cart moving at 4 m/s collides and sticks to an identical stationary cart. The final speed is\nA) 1 m/s  B) 2 m/s  C) 4 m/s  D) 8 m/s\n\n**9.** A 10 N force acts for 3.0 s on a 2.0 kg object initially at rest. Its final speed is\nA) 5 m/s  B) 6 m/s  C) 15 m/s  D) 30 m/s\n\n**10.** The moment of inertia of a uniform rod (mass M, length L) about one end is\nA) M L^2/12  B) M L^2/3  C) M L^2/2  D) M L^2\n\n**11.** For a solid sphere rolling without slipping, the fraction of the kinetic energy that is rotational is\nA) 2/5  B) 2/7  C) 1/2  D) 5/7\n\n**12.** The ratio of escape speed to circular orbital speed at the same radius is\nA) 1  B) sqrt 2  C) 2  D) 1/sqrt 2\n\n**13.** For circular orbits around the same planet, orbital speed varies with radius r as\nA) r  B) 1/r  C) 1/sqrt r  D) r^2\n\n**14.** For SHM, at maximum displacement the\nA) speed is maximum  B) acceleration is zero  C) speed is zero and acceleration is maximum  D) kinetic energy is maximum\n\n**15.** A damped oscillator is critically damped when\nA) b = 0  B) γ = ω0  C) γ > ω0  D) γ < ω0\n\n**16.** A driven lightly damped oscillator has maximum amplitude when the drive frequency is\nA) very small  B) close to the natural frequency  C) very large  D) twice the natural frequency\n\n**17.** If the tension in a string is quadrupled, the wave speed\nA) quadruples  B) doubles  C) halves  D) is unchanged\n\n**18.** A pipe closed at one end supports\nA) all harmonics  B) only odd harmonics  C) only even harmonics  D) no standing waves\n\n**19.** A source of sound approaches a stationary listener. The observed frequency is\nA) lower than the source frequency  B) the same  C) higher  D) zero\n\n**20.** A sound level of 40 dB corresponds to an intensity of\nA) 10^-4 W/m^2  B) 10^-8 W/m^2  C) 10^-12 W/m^2  D) 40 W/m^2\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** A ball rolls off a table 1.2 m high at 3.0 m/s horizontally. Find the time of flight, the horizontal distance and the impact speed.\n\n**B2. (5)** A 2.0 kg block rests on a frictionless 30° incline, held by a spring (k = 100 N/m) parallel to the slope. Find the spring's extension at equilibrium.\n\n**B3. (5)** A 1000 kg car travelling at 20 m/s brakes to a stop with the wheels locked (μ_k = 0.80). Find the stopping distance and the work done by friction.\n\n**B4. (5)** A 60 kg person jumps horizontally off a 120 kg stationary boat at 3.0 m/s relative to the water. Find the boat's recoil speed and the total kinetic energy produced.\n\n**B5. (5)** A planet has 1/10 of Earth's mass and 1/2 of Earth's radius. Find the surface gravitational acceleration and the weight of a 70 kg astronaut there.\n\n**B6. (5)** A 0.20 kg ball on a string moves in a horizontal circle of radius 0.50 m at 2.0 m/s on a frictionless table. The string is pulled slowly through a hole until the radius is 0.25 m. Find the new speed, the change in kinetic energy and the work done.\n\n**B7. (5)** A mass on a spring (k = 80 N/m) oscillates as x = 0.050 cos(20 t). Find the mass, the maximum speed and the total energy.\n\n**B8. (5)** A wave is y = 0.010 sin(10 x + 200 t) (SI). Find its direction, wavelength, frequency and speed.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1.** A 5.0 kg block lies on a 30° incline (μ_k = 0.10), connected by a light rope over a frictionless pulley at the top to a 4.0 kg mass hanging vertically.\n(a) Draw the free-body diagrams and decide the direction of motion. [2]\n(b) Find the acceleration of the system. [3]\n(c) Find the tension in the rope. [2]\n(d) Find the speed of the system after it moves 2.0 m from rest. [2]\n(e) Check (d) using the work-energy theorem. [1]\n\n**C2.** A 0.50 kg block compresses a spring (k = 800 N/m) by 0.15 m and is released on a track that is frictionless except for a 1.0 m rough section (μ_k = 0.40).\n(a) Find the energy stored in the spring. [2]\n(b) Find the speed after crossing the rough section. [3]\n(c) How high does it rise up a frictionless ramp beyond the rough section? [2]\n(d) If instead the block entered a frictionless vertical loop of radius 0.50 m after the rough section, would it complete the loop? Justify. [3]\n\n**C3.** A uniform rod of mass 2.0 kg and length 1.0 m is pivoted at one end and released from rest in the horizontal position.\n(a) Find the moment of inertia about the pivot. [1]\n(b) Find the angular acceleration at release. [2]\n(c) Find the force exerted by the pivot at the moment of release. [3]\n(d) Find the angular speed and the tip speed when the rod is vertical. [3]\n(e) Compare the tip speed with the speed of a ball dropped through the same 1.0 m, and explain the difference. [1]\n\n**C4.** A geostationary satellite has a period equal to one sidereal day (86,164 s).\n(a) Find the radius of its orbit and its altitude. [4]\n(b) Find its orbital speed. [2]\n(c) Find the minimum energy needed to place a 1000 kg satellite in that orbit from the surface (ignore Earth's rotation and drag). [3]\n(d) Why must it orbit above the equator? [1]\n\n**C5.** A guitar string of length 0.65 m, linear density 0.0020 kg/m, and tension 80 N is plucked.\n(a) Find the wave speed and the fundamental frequency, and the frequency of the third harmonic. [3]\n(b) A 0.50 W point source of sound at this frequency is 10 m from a listener. Find the intensity and the level in dB. [3]\n(c) The listener walks towards the source at 10 m/s. Find the frequency heard. [2]\n(d) Find the length of a pipe closed at one end whose fundamental matches the string's fundamental. [2]\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **C** · 2 **B** · 3 **B** · 4 **C** · 5 **B** · 6 **C** · 7 **B** · 8 **B** · 9 **C** · 10 **B** · 11 **B** · 12 **B** · 13 **C** · 14 **C** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **B** · 18 **B** · 19 **C** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons:\n1. v = 6t - 2 = 10.\n2. (1/2)(9.8)(16) = 78.4 m.\n3. Only the vertical component vanishes.\n4. v^2/r = 20.\n5. f_k = 0.3(98) = 29.4 N.\n6. m g h = 98 J (positive work, since the force and displacement are both downward).\n7. P = W/t.\n8. Momentum: 16 = 8v, so v = 2.\n9. J = 30 N s, v = 15 m/s.\n10. M L^2/3.\n11. (2/5)/(1 + 2/5) = 2/7.\n12. sqrt(2 G M/r)/sqrt(G M/r).\n13. v = sqrt(G M/r).\n14. At x = A, v = 0 and a = ω^2 A.\n15. Critical damping.\n16. Resonance.\n17. v ∝ sqrt(T).\n18. Node at the closed end, antinode at the open end.\n19. Doppler shift upward.\n20. β = 10 log(I/10^-12) = 40, so I = 10^-8.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** Step 1: t = sqrt(2h/g) = sqrt(2 × 1.2/9.8) = sqrt(0.245) = **0.495 s**.\nStep 2: x = v t = 3.0 × 0.495 = **1.48 m**.\nStep 3: v_y = g t = 4.85 m/s, so impact speed = sqrt(3.0² + 4.85²) = **5.70 m/s** (check: sqrt(9 + 2 × 9.8 × 1.2) = 5.70 ✓).\n\n**B2.** Equilibrium along the slope: k x = m g sin 30°, so x = (2.0 × 9.8 × 0.5)/100 = 9.8/100 = **0.098 m (9.8 cm)**.\n\n**B3.** Step 1: a = μ g = 7.84 m/s² (μ = 0.80).\nStep 2: d = v²/(2a) = 400/15.68 = **25.5 m**.\nStep 3: W_friction = -(1/2) m v² = -(1/2)(1000)(400) = **-2.0 × 10^5 J**.\n\n**B4.** Step 1: momentum: 60(3.0) = 120 v_boat, so the boat recoils at **1.5 m/s** (in the opposite direction).\nStep 2: kinetic energy: (1/2)(60)(9) + (1/2)(120)(2.25) = 270 + 135 = **405 J** (supplied by the person's muscles).\n\n**B5.** Step 1: g' = G M'/R'² = g (1/10)/(1/4) = 0.4 g = **3.92 m/s²**.\nStep 2: weight = 70 × 3.92 = **274 N** (versus 686 N on Earth).\n\n**B6.** Step 1: the tension is central, so angular momentum is conserved: m v r = m v' r', so v' = v (r/r') = 2.0 × 2 = **4.0 m/s**.\nStep 2: K_i = (1/2)(0.2)(4) = 0.40 J, K_f = (1/2)(0.2)(16) = 1.60 J.\nStep 3: **ΔK = +1.20 J**, which equals the work done by the person pulling the string.\n\n**B7.** Step 1: ω = 20 rad/s, A = 0.050 m. m = k/ω² = 80/400 = **0.20 kg**.\nStep 2: v_max = A ω = 0.050 × 20 = **1.0 m/s**.\nStep 3: E = (1/2) k A² = (1/2)(80)(0.0025) = **0.10 J** (check: (1/2)(0.2)(1) = 0.10 ✓).\n\n**B8.** Because x and t have the same sign, the wave travels in the **-x direction**. k = 10 rad/m so **λ = 2π/10 = 0.628 m**. ω = 200 rad/s so **f = 31.8 Hz**. **v = ω/k = 20 m/s** (check: f λ = 31.8 · 0.628 = 20 ✓).\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.** m1 = 5.0 kg on the incline, m2 = 4.0 kg hanging.\n(a) The hanging weight is 39.2 N. The down-slope component on m1 is 5(9.8)(0.5) = 24.5 N, and the maximum friction is 0.10 · 5 · 9.8 cos 30° = 4.24 N. Since 39.2 > 24.5 + 4.24, the hanging mass descends and the block **moves up the slope**; kinetic friction acts down the slope. (1 mark each for the free-body diagrams and the decision.)\n(b) Net driving force = 39.2 - 24.5 - 4.24 = 10.46 N. a = 10.46/(5 + 4) = **1.16 m/s^2**.\n(c) For m2: m2 g - T = m2 a, so T = 4(9.8 - 1.162) = **34.6 N**. Check on m1: T - 24.5 - 4.24 = 5.81 = 5 a ✓.\n(d) v = sqrt(2 a d) = sqrt(2 · 1.162 · 2.0) = **2.16 m/s**.\n(e) Step 1: Work by gravity on m2: 4(9.8)(2) = 78.4 J.\nStep 2: Gravity on m1: -5(9.8)(1.0) = -49 J (it rises 2 sin 30° = 1 m).\nStep 3: Friction: -4.24 · 2 = -8.49 J.\nStep 4: Net = 20.9 J = (1/2)(9)(2.156)^2 = 20.9 J ✓.\n\n**C2.**\n(a) U = (1/2) k x^2 = (1/2)(800)(0.0225) = **9.0 J**.\n(b) Friction loss = μ m g d = 0.40 · 0.5 · 9.8 · 1.0 = 1.96 J. K = 9.0 - 1.96 = 7.04 J, so v = sqrt(2 · 7.04/0.5) = **5.31 m/s**.\n(c) h = K/(m g) = 7.04/(0.5 · 9.8) = **1.44 m**.\n(d) Step 1: A loop of radius R is completed if the kinetic energy at the bottom is at least (5/2) m g R = 2.5(0.5)(9.8)(0.5) = 6.125 J.\nStep 2: Since 7.04 J > 6.125 J, **yes, it completes the loop** (with 0.9 J to spare).\nStep 3: At the top, v^2 = v_bottom^2 - 4 g R = 28.16 - 19.6 = 8.56 m^2/s^2 ≥ g R = 4.9 ✓.\n\n**C3.** M = 2.0 kg, L = 1.0 m.\n(a) I = M L^2/3 = **0.667 kg m^2**.\n(b) Torque at release = M g (L/2) = 9.8 N m. α = τ/I = 9.8/0.667 = **14.7 rad/s^2** (= 3g/(2L)).\n(c) Acceleration of the centre of mass at release = α L/2 = 7.35 m/s^2 downward (no centripetal term as ω = 0). Then M g - N = M a_cm, so N = 2(9.8 - 7.35) = **4.9 N upward** (a quarter of the weight).\n(d) Energy: M g L/2 = (1/2) I ω^2, so ω = sqrt(3g/L) = sqrt(29.4) = **5.42 rad/s**. Tip speed = ω L = **5.42 m/s**.\n(e) A ball dropped through the same 1.0 m would reach sqrt(2 g L) = 4.43 m/s, slower than the tip (5.42 m/s). A rigid rod must share a single angular acceleration, so the centre of mass accelerates at only 0.75 g (7.35 m/s^2) while the tip, twice as far from the pivot, accelerates at 1.5 g (14.7 m/s^2), faster than free fall.\n\n**C4.**\n(a) Step 1: T = 2π sqrt(r³/(G M)), so r³ = G M T²/(4π²) = 3.986 × 10^14 × (86,164)²/39.478 = 7.50 × 10^22 m³.\nStep 2: **r = 4.216 × 10^7 m**.\nStep 3: altitude = r - R_E = **3.58 × 10^7 m (35,800 km)**.\n(b) v = 2π r/T = sqrt(G M/r) = **3.07 km/s**.\n(c) ΔE = m [G M/R_E - G M/(2r)] = 1000[6.256 × 10^7 - 4.73 × 10^6] = 1000 · 5.78 × 10^7 = **5.78 × 10^10 J**.\n(d) The orbit's plane must contain Earth's centre. Only an equatorial orbit can keep a satellite fixed over one point on the ground (any inclination would make it trace a figure-eight).\n\n**C5.**\n(a) Step 1: v = sqrt(T/μ) = sqrt(80/0.0020) = sqrt(40,000) = **200 m/s**.\nStep 2: f1 = v/(2L) = 200/(2 × 0.65) = 200/1.3 = **153.8 Hz**.\nStep 3: f3 = 3 f1 = **461.5 Hz**.\n(b) I = P/(4π r^2) = 0.50/(4π · 100) = **3.98 × 10^-4 W/m^2**. β = 10 log10(3.98 × 10^-4/10^-12) = 10(8.60) = **86.0 dB**.\n(c) Observer approaching a stationary source: f' = f (v + v_o)/v = 153.8 · 353/343 = **158.3 Hz**.\n(d) Closed pipe fundamental f = v/(4L), so L = 343/(4 · 153.8) = **0.557 m**.\n","notes":"# Physics I (Mechanics and Waves): Complete Lecture Notes\n\nConventions: SI units throughout. g = 9.8 m/s^2 unless stated. Vectors are written in components. \"Δ\" means \"change in\". Use the structured problem method: (1) draw a diagram, (2) choose axes and a sign convention, (3) list knowns and unknowns, (4) write the governing laws, (5) solve symbolically, (6) substitute numbers and check units and limiting cases.\n\n## Chapter 1. Measurement, Units and Vectors\n\n### 1.1 Units and dimensional analysis\nSI base units: metre (m), kilogram (kg), second (s), ampere (A), kelvin (K), mole, candela. Every physical equation must be dimensionally consistent. Example: the period of a pendulum T has dimension [T]. If T depends on L [L], g [L T^-2] and m [M], then T = k sqrt(L/g) and m cannot appear. Dimensional analysis fixes the form but not the constant k (here 2π).\n\n**Significant figures and uncertainty.** Report results to the precision of the least precise input. For products and quotients add relative uncertainties; for sums add absolute uncertainties.\n\n### 1.2 Vectors\nA vector has magnitude and direction. Components: A = (A_x, A_y) with A_x = A cos θ, A_y = A sin θ, |A| = sqrt(A_x^2 + A_y^2), tan θ = A_y/A_x. Add vectors component by component.\n- **Dot product:** A · B = AB cos φ = A_x B_x + A_y B_y + A_z B_z. Gives work and projections.\n- **Cross product:** |A × B| = AB sin φ, direction by the right-hand rule. Gives torque and angular momentum. Components: (A_y B_z - A_z B_y, A_z B_x - A_x B_z, A_x B_y - A_y B_x).\n\n## Chapter 2. Kinematics in One Dimension\n\nPosition x(t); velocity v = dx/dt; acceleration a = dv/dt. Average velocity = Δx/Δt; speed is the magnitude of velocity. Displacement is not distance travelled.\n\n### 2.1 Constant acceleration\n- v = v0 + a t\n- x = x0 + v0 t + (1/2) a t^2\n- v^2 = v0^2 + 2a(x - x0)\n- x - x0 = (1/2)(v0 + v) t\n\n**Example.** A car brakes from 30 m/s at 6 m/s^2 in the opposite direction. Stopping time t = 30/6 = 5 s; stopping distance = 30^2/(2·6) = 75 m.\n\n### 2.2 Free fall\nNeglecting air resistance, a = -g (taking up as positive). A ball thrown upward at v0 reaches maximum height v0^2/(2g) after v0/g seconds. It returns to the launch height with speed v0.\n\n### 2.3 Calculus approach\nIf a(t) is given, v(t) = v0 + ∫ a dt and x(t) = x0 + ∫ v dt. Areas under the v-t graph give displacement, and slopes give acceleration.\n\n### 2.4 Chase problems\nWrite each object's position as a function of the same clock, set them equal and solve. Check which root is physical.\n\n## Chapter 3. Motion in Two and Three Dimensions\n\n### 3.1 Projectile motion\nHorizontal and vertical motions are independent: a_x = 0, a_y = -g. With launch speed v0 at angle θ:\n- x = v0 cos θ · t, y = v0 sin θ · t - (1/2) g t^2\n- Time of flight (level ground) = 2 v0 sin θ / g\n- Range R = v0^2 sin 2θ / g, maximum at θ = 45°\n- Maximum height H = v0^2 sin^2 θ / (2g)\n- Trajectory: y = x tan θ - g x^2/(2 v0^2 cos^2 θ), a parabola.\n\n**Example.** v0 = 25 m/s at 40°: time 3.28 s, range 62.8 m, maximum height 13.2 m. For range 50 m: sin 2θ = 50 · 9.8/625 = 0.784, so θ = 25.8° or 64.2°. Complementary angles give equal ranges.\n\n### 3.2 Relative velocity\nv_(A rel C) = v_(A rel B) + v_(B rel C). Boat in a river: heading straight across takes the shortest time (width/boat speed) but drifts downstream; to land directly opposite, aim upstream at sin φ = v_river/v_boat.\n\n### 3.3 Circular motion\nUniform circular motion at radius r and speed v: centripetal acceleration a_c = v^2/r = ω^2 r, directed to the centre, with period T = 2πr/v = 2π/ω. The net inward force is m v^2/r. It is a requirement on the net force, not a new force. Non-uniform circular motion adds a tangential acceleration a_t = dv/dt = r α.\n\n## Chapter 4. Newton's Laws and Applications\n\n### 4.1 The laws\n1. An object stays at rest or in uniform motion unless acted on by a net force (inertial frames).\n2. ΣF = m a (vector equation, applied component by component). More generally ΣF = dp/dt.\n3. Forces come in equal and opposite pairs acting on **different** bodies.\n\n### 4.2 Common forces\n- **Weight** W = m g, directed downward.\n- **Normal force** N, perpendicular to the surface; found from the equations, not assumed equal to mg.\n- **Tension** T along a rope; the same throughout a massless rope over a frictionless pulley.\n- **Friction:** static f_s ≤ μ_s N, kinetic f_k = μ_k N (μ_k < μ_s). It opposes relative (or impending) sliding.\n- **Spring** (Hooke): F = -k x.\n- **Drag:** F = -b v (low speed) or -(1/2) C ρ A v^2 (high speed). Terminal velocity occurs when drag equals weight: v_t = m g/b for linear drag.\n\n### 4.3 Problem patterns\n**Incline.** For angle θ, the component of gravity along the slope is m g sin θ and perpendicular is m g cos θ. N = m g cos θ. Sliding down with kinetic friction: a = g(sin θ - μ_k cos θ). At θ = 30°, μ_k = 0.2: a = 3.20 m/s^2.\n\n**Atwood machine.** Masses m1 < m2 over a pulley: a = (m2 - m1)g/(m1 + m2), T = 2 m1 m2 g/(m1 + m2). For 3 kg and 5 kg: a = 2.45 m/s^2, T = 36.75 N.\n\n**Apparent weight.** In an elevator with upward acceleration a, N = m(g + a). In free fall N = 0.\n\n**Banked curve** without friction: tan θ = v^2/(r g). With friction on a flat curve, the maximum speed is v = sqrt(μ_s g r).\n\n**Connected bodies.** Draw a free-body diagram for each body; use the same acceleration magnitude along the connection.\n\n## Chapter 5. Work, Energy and Power\n\n### 5.1 Work\nW = ∫ F · dr. For a constant force, W = F d cos φ. Work done by a spring from x1 to x2: -(1/2)k(x2^2 - x1^2). **Work-energy theorem:** W_net = ΔK, with K = (1/2) m v^2.\n\n### 5.2 Conservative forces and potential energy\nA force is conservative if the work it does around any closed path is zero (gravity, spring, electrostatic). Then F = -dU/dx. Gravitational U = m g h (near the surface), spring U = (1/2) k x^2, universal gravitation U = -G M m/r.\n\n**Conservation of mechanical energy:** if only conservative forces do work, K + U is constant. With non-conservative work W_nc: K_i + U_i + W_nc = K_f + U_f. Friction contributes W_nc = -f d.\n\n**Examples.**\n- A 2 kg block slides from rest down a frictionless curve of height 1.5 m: v = sqrt(2 g h) = 5.42 m/s. On a rough floor with μ = 0.3 it stops after d = h/μ = 5 m.\n- A spring (k = 200 N/m) compressed 0.1 m launches a 0.5 kg block at v = x sqrt(k/m) = 2 m/s.\n- To just complete a vertical loop of radius R, the cart must start from a height 2.5 R above the bottom (at the top v^2 ≥ g R).\n\n### 5.3 Energy diagrams\nEquilibrium points are where dU/dx = 0: stable at minima, unstable at maxima. The turning points are where E = U.\n\n### 5.4 Power\nP = dW/dt = F · v. Units: watt (W). Average power = W/Δt. Example: lifting 500 kg at 0.5 m/s needs P = 500 · 9.8 · 0.5 = 2450 W.\n\n## Chapter 6. Momentum, Impulse and Collisions\n\n### 6.1 Momentum and impulse\np = m v. Impulse J = ∫ F dt = Δp. For a system with no external net force, total momentum is conserved.\n\n### 6.2 Collisions\n- **Elastic:** momentum and kinetic energy conserved. In 1D, the relative velocity reverses: v1 - v2 = -(v1' - v2'). Solutions: v1' = ((m1 - m2)/(m1 + m2)) v1 + (2 m2/(m1 + m2)) v2, and v2' = (2 m1/(m1 + m2)) v1 + ((m2 - m1)/(m1 + m2)) v2.\n- **Perfectly inelastic:** the bodies stick, v' = (m1 v1 + m2 v2)/(m1 + m2); this loses the most kinetic energy.\n- **Equal masses elastic in 2D** (one at rest): the final velocities are perpendicular.\n\n**Example.** 2 kg at 6 m/s hits a 4 kg mass at rest: elastic gives v1' = -2 m/s, v2' = 4 m/s. Perfectly inelastic gives v' = 2 m/s, losing 24 J of the initial 36 J.\n\n**Ballistic pendulum.** Momentum conservation in the collision (inelastic), then energy conservation in the swing: v_bullet = ((m + M)/m) sqrt(2 g h).\n\n### 6.3 Centre of mass\nr_cm = Σ m_i r_i / M. Its acceleration is ΣF_ext/M, regardless of internal forces. Rocket propulsion: m dv/dt = v_ex dm/dt gives v = v_ex ln(m_i/m_f).\n\n## Chapter 7. Rotational Dynamics\n\n### 7.1 Kinematics\nθ, ω = dθ/dt, α = dω/dt. For constant α: ω = ω0 + α t, θ = ω0 t + (1/2) α t^2. Linking: s = r θ, v = r ω, a_t = r α.\n\n### 7.2 Torque, moment of inertia, Newton's second law for rotation\nTorque τ = r × F (magnitude r F sin φ). Moment of inertia I = Σ m_i r_i^2 = ∫ r^2 dm. **Στ = I α.** Rotational kinetic energy (1/2) I ω^2.\nCommon results (axis through the centre unless noted): thin hoop M R^2; solid disc or cylinder (1/2) M R^2; solid sphere (2/5) M R^2; thin rod (1/12) M L^2 (about the centre), (1/3) M L^2 (about one end).\n**Parallel axis theorem:** I = I_cm + M d^2.\n\n### 7.3 Rolling without slipping\nv_cm = R ω. Total kinetic energy = (1/2) M v^2 + (1/2) I_cm ω^2. A solid cylinder released from height h reaches the bottom with v = sqrt(4 g h/3) (3.96 m/s for h = 1.2 m), slower than a sliding block because part of the energy goes into rotation. Acceleration down an incline: a = g sin θ/(1 + I/(M R^2)).\n\n### 7.4 Angular momentum\nL = I ω (about a fixed axis); in general L = r × p. dL/dt = Στ. With no net external torque, L is conserved. Example: a skater's moment of inertia changes from 4 to 1.5 kg m^2 with initial ω = 2 rad/s: ω' = 5.33 rad/s, and the arms do 13.3 J of work (kinetic energy rises from 8 J to 21.3 J).\n\n### 7.5 Static equilibrium\nΣ F = 0 and Σ τ = 0 about any point. Choose the pivot to eliminate unknown forces.\n\n## Chapter 8. Gravitation\n\nNewton's law: F = G M m/r^2, with G = 6.674 × 10^-11 N m^2/kg^2. Surface gravity g = G M_E/R_E^2 = 9.8 m/s^2.\n- **Circular orbit:** v = sqrt(G M/r), period T = 2π sqrt(r^3/(G M)). (Kepler's third law: T^2 ∝ r^3.) At 400 km altitude, v = 7.67 km/s and T = 92.4 minutes.\n- **Escape speed:** v_esc = sqrt(2 G M/R) = 11.2 km/s for Earth.\n- **Orbital energy:** E = -G M m/(2r) for a circular orbit. Bound orbits have E < 0.\n- **Kepler's laws:** (1) orbits are ellipses with the Sun at one focus; (2) equal areas in equal times (angular momentum conservation); (3) T^2 ∝ a^3.\n\n## Chapter 9. Oscillations\n\n### 9.1 Simple harmonic motion\nA restoring force F = -k x gives x'' = -ω^2 x with ω = sqrt(k/m). Solution x = A cos(ω t + φ). Period T = 2π/ω, frequency f = 1/T.\n- v = -A ω sin(ω t + φ), a = -ω^2 x.\n- v_max = A ω, a_max = A ω^2, and v = ω sqrt(A^2 - x^2).\n- Total energy E = (1/2) k A^2 = (1/2) m v^2 + (1/2) k x^2 is constant.\n- Amplitude and phase from initial conditions: A = sqrt(x0^2 + (v0/ω)^2).\n\n**Examples.**\n- Mass-spring: m = 0.4 kg, k = 40 N/m gives ω = 10 rad/s and T = 0.628 s.\n- Simple pendulum (small angles): ω = sqrt(g/L), T = 2π sqrt(L/g). For L = 1.00 m, T = 2.007 s. A \"seconds pendulum\" (T = 2 s) has L = 0.993 m.\n- Physical pendulum: T = 2π sqrt(I/(m g d)).\n- Vertical spring: the equilibrium is shifted by m g/k but the frequency is unchanged.\n- Springs in series: 1/k = 1/k1 + 1/k2; in parallel: k = k1 + k2.\n\n### 9.2 Damped oscillations\nWith drag -b v: m x'' + b x' + k x = 0. Define γ = b/(2m) and ω0 = sqrt(k/m).\n- **Underdamped** (γ < ω0): x = A e^(-γ t) cos(ω' t + φ), with ω' = sqrt(ω0^2 - γ^2). Energy decays as e^(-2γ t).\n- **Critically damped** (γ = ω0): the fastest return to equilibrium without oscillation.\n- **Overdamped** (γ > ω0): slow monotonic decay.\n- Quality factor Q = ω0/(2γ) = sqrt(k m)/b.\n\n### 9.3 Driven oscillations and resonance\nFor a force F0 cos(ω t): steady-state amplitude A(ω) = (F0/m)/sqrt((ω0^2 - ω^2)^2 + (2 γ ω)^2). Resonance occurs near ω ≈ ω0, where the amplitude is about F0/(2 m γ ω0) and the peak sharpens as damping decreases. Phase: the response lags the drive by 0 (low frequency) to π (high frequency), and by π/2 at resonance.\nEngineering relevance: bridges, buildings, MEMS resonators, filters (RLC circuits are the same equation).\n\n## Chapter 10. Mechanical Waves\n\n### 10.1 Travelling waves\nA wave is a disturbance propagating without net transport of matter. For a sinusoidal wave y(x, t) = A sin(k x - ω t + φ):\n- wavenumber k = 2π/λ, angular frequency ω = 2π f, speed v = f λ = ω/k.\n- A wave y = f(x - v t) moves right; f(x + v t) moves left.\n- Particle velocity ∂y/∂t is different from wave speed; max transverse speed = A ω.\n\n**Wave equation:** ∂²y/∂x² = (1/v²) ∂²y/∂t². **String:** v = sqrt(T/μ) (tension over linear density). **Sound in a gas:** v = sqrt(B/ρ), about 343 m/s in air at 20 °C.\n**Power:** a sinusoidal string wave transmits P = (1/2) μ ω² A² v.\n\n**Example.** y = 0.02 sin(4π x - 100π t): λ = 0.5 m, f = 50 Hz, v = 25 m/s, max transverse speed 6.28 m/s. For μ = 0.01 kg/m, T = μ v² = 6.25 N and P = 4.93 W.\n\n### 10.2 Superposition and interference\nWaves add linearly. Two identical waves with phase difference δ give amplitude 2A cos(δ/2). Constructive: path difference Δ = n λ. Destructive: Δ = (n + 1/2) λ. A phase shift of π occurs on reflection from a fixed end (none from a free end).\n**Beats:** two frequencies f1, f2 give a beat frequency |f1 - f2| (440 Hz and 444 Hz beat at 4 Hz).\n\n### 10.3 Standing waves\nCounter-propagating equal waves produce y = 2A sin(k x) cos(ω t), with nodes and antinodes.\n- **String fixed at both ends** (length L): λ_n = 2L/n, f_n = n v/(2L), n = 1, 2, 3, ...\n- **Pipe open at both ends:** f_n = n v/(2L).\n- **Pipe closed at one end:** f_n = n v/(4L), odd n only.\nExample: a 0.8 m string with v = 200 m/s has harmonics 125, 250, 375 Hz. A 0.5 m open pipe resonates at 343 Hz, but a closed one at 171.5 Hz (and odd multiples).\n\n### 10.4 Sound\n- **Intensity:** I = P/A. For a point source, I = P/(4π r²) (inverse-square law).\n- **Intensity level:** β = 10 log10(I/I0) dB, I0 = 10^-12 W/m². Doubling the distance lowers the level by 6 dB; doubling the intensity raises it by 3 dB.\n- **Doppler effect:** f' = f (v ± v_obs)/(v ∓ v_src), with the upper signs when the observer or source approaches. Example: a 700 Hz siren approaching at 30 m/s is heard at 700 · 343/313 = 767 Hz and receding at 644 Hz.\n- **Shock waves:** sin θ = v/v_src (Mach cone) for supersonic sources.\n\n## Chapter 11. Common Mistakes Checklist\n1. Mixing distance with displacement, or forgetting sign conventions (in free fall, a = -g if up is positive).\n2. Assuming N = m g on an incline (it is m g cos θ) or in an accelerating elevator.\n3. Treating centripetal force as an extra force on the free-body diagram.\n4. Applying conservation of mechanical energy when friction or an inelastic collision is present.\n5. Using conservation of kinetic energy in an inelastic collision. Momentum is conserved, kinetic energy is not.\n6. Forgetting the factor of 2 in escape speed or the 1/2 in kinetic energy.\n7. Confusing wave speed with particle speed, and using f = v/λ with the wrong medium.\n8. Using degrees with ω t in a calculator set to radians (or vice versa).\n","plan":"# Physics I (Mechanics and Waves): 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 11 hours per week (3 reading, 5 to 6 problems, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Physics rewards problem-solving practice far more than re-reading: aim for at least 8 full problems per week.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Young and Freedman, *University Physics with Modern Physics* (Pearson). The standard engineering text with graded problems.\n- Halliday, Resnick and Walker, *Fundamentals of Physics*. Clear explanations and many conceptual questions.\n- Kleppner and Kolenkow, *An Introduction to Mechanics*. Harder and deeper; use for the second pass.\n- Feynman, *Lectures on Physics* Volume I (free online). For insight, not drill.\n\n## Free courses\n- MIT OpenCourseWare 8.01SC Classical Mechanics (lectures, problem sets, exams with solutions).\n- MIT OpenCourseWare 8.03SC Physics III: Vibrations and Waves (for the wave chapters).\n- Walter Lewin's 8.01 lectures (demonstrations that build intuition).\n- PhET interactive simulations (University of Colorado) for projectiles, springs, waves and collisions.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Units, vectors, 1D kinematics (Ch 1, 2).** Objectives: use dimensional analysis; solve constant-acceleration problems. Do: Assignment 1 Q1, Q2. Checkpoint: derive the four kinematic equations from a = constant.\n\n**Week 2: 2D kinematics, projectiles, relative motion, circular motion (Ch 3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q3, Q4, Q7.\n\n**Week 3: Newton's laws and friction (Ch 4.1 to 4.3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q5, Q6, Q8. Habit: a free-body diagram for every problem.\n\n**Week 4: Dynamics review, drag and terminal velocity (Ch 4).** Sit the **Class Test** (60 minutes). Fix every missed question before moving on.\n\n**Week 5: Work, energy and power (Ch 5).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1 to Q3. Checkpoint: solve a loop-the-loop problem unaided.\n\n**Week 6: Momentum, impulse and collisions (Ch 6).** Do: Assignment 2 Q4 to Q6.\n\n**Week 7: Rotation: kinematics, torque, moment of inertia (Ch 7.1 to 7.3).** Do: Assignment 2 Q7. Checkpoint: derive I for a rod and a disc by integration.\n\n**Week 8: Angular momentum, equilibrium, gravitation (Ch 7.4, 7.5, 8).** Do: Assignment 2 Q8 to Q10. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 9: Simple harmonic motion (Ch 9.1).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1, Q2. Use a stopwatch to measure your own pendulum and compare with 2π sqrt(L/g).\n\n**Week 10: Damped and driven oscillations, resonance (Ch 9.2, 9.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q3, Q4. Plot A(ω) for three values of the damping.\n\n**Week 11: Travelling waves and the wave equation (Ch 10.1).** Do: Assignment 3 Q5.\n\n**Week 12: Superposition, interference, standing waves (Ch 10.2, 10.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q6, Q9.\n\n**Week 13: Sound, intensity, Doppler effect (Ch 10.4).** Do: Assignment 3 Q7, Q8.\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, formula sheet only).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can solve constant-acceleration and projectile problems with correct sign conventions.\n- [ ] I can draw a correct free-body diagram and solve connected-body problems with friction.\n- [ ] I can choose between force, energy and momentum methods for a problem.\n- [ ] I can analyse 1D and 2D collisions.\n- [ ] I can use torque, moment of inertia and angular momentum conservation.\n- [ ] I can compute orbital speeds, periods and energies.\n- [ ] I can write and fit an SHM solution, and explain damping and resonance.\n- [ ] I can analyse standing waves on strings and in pipes, and apply the Doppler effect.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with a formula sheet only.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Solve symbolically first, then plug in numbers. Check units and limiting cases (what if m → 0, or θ → 0?).\n2. Always ask whether the result is plausible (a car's stopping distance of 3 km is a red flag).\n3. Redo each example problem a week later with the solution covered.\n4. Verify a few problems numerically in Python (scipy.integrate.solve_ivp) to see the physics evolve.\n5. Keep a one-page formula sheet that you write yourself.\n","test":"# Physics I (Mechanics and Waves): Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed. Use g = 9.8 m/s^2 and v_sound = 343 m/s. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each).\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** A ball is thrown straight up at 20 m/s. The time to reach the highest point is about\nA) 1.0 s  B) 2.0 s  C) 4.0 s  D) 20 s\n\n**2.** A car accelerates uniformly from rest to 20 m/s in 5 s. The distance travelled is\nA) 20 m  B) 50 m  C) 100 m  D) 200 m\n\n**3.** On level ground and neglecting air resistance, the launch angle giving maximum range is\nA) 30°  B) 45°  C) 60°  D) 90°\n\n**4.** A net force of 10 N acts on a 2 kg object. Its acceleration is\nA) 0.2 m/s^2  B) 5 m/s^2  C) 12 m/s^2  D) 20 m/s^2\n\n**5.** The weight of a 5 kg object on Earth is about\nA) 5 N  B) 9.8 N  C) 49 N  D) 0.51 N\n\n**6.** A block slides down a frictionless 30° incline. Its acceleration is\nA) 9.8 m/s^2  B) 8.5 m/s^2  C) 4.9 m/s^2  D) 0\n\n**7.** The work done lifting a 10 kg mass 2 m at constant speed is about\nA) 20 J  B) 98 J  C) 196 J  D) 2000 J\n\n**8.** The kinetic energy of a 2 kg object moving at 3 m/s is\nA) 3 J  B) 6 J  C) 9 J  D) 18 J\n\n**9.** A spring with k = 100 N/m is stretched 0.20 m. The stored energy is\nA) 2 J  B) 4 J  C) 10 J  D) 20 J\n\n**10.** Total momentum is conserved\nA) only in elastic collisions  B) in all collisions when external forces are negligible  C) only in inelastic collisions  D) never in collisions\n\n**11.** A moving ball makes a head-on elastic collision with an identical stationary ball. Afterwards\nA) both move at half speed  B) the first stops and the second moves with the original speed  C) both stop  D) the first rebounds\n\n**12.** The moment of inertia of a thin hoop of mass M and radius R about its axis is\nA) (1/2) M R^2  B) (2/5) M R^2  C) M R^2  D) (1/12) M R^2\n\n**13.** A torque of 3 N m acts on a body with I = 0.5 kg m^2. The angular acceleration is\nA) 1.5 rad/s^2  B) 3.5 rad/s^2  C) 6 rad/s^2  D) 0.17 rad/s^2\n\n**14.** Angular momentum of a system is conserved when\nA) kinetic energy is constant  B) the net external torque is zero  C) the net external force is zero only  D) the speed is constant\n\n**15.** If a satellite's orbital radius doubles, its period increases by a factor of\nA) 2  B) 2.83  C) 4  D) 8\n\n**16.** If the mass on a spring is quadrupled, the period\nA) quadruples  B) doubles  C) halves  D) is unchanged\n\n**17.** If the length of a simple pendulum is quadrupled, the period\nA) quadruples  B) doubles  C) halves  D) is unchanged\n\n**18.** A wave has frequency 100 Hz and wavelength 0.5 m. Its speed is\nA) 50 m/s  B) 200 m/s  C) 0.005 m/s  D) 100.5 m/s\n\n**19.** The fundamental standing wave on a string fixed at both ends of length L has wavelength\nA) L/2  B) L  C) 2L  D) 4L\n\n**20.** Increasing sound intensity by a factor of 10 raises the intensity level by\nA) 1 dB  B) 3 dB  C) 10 dB  D) 100 dB\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (10 marks)** A 5.0 kg box is pulled across a floor (μ_k = 0.20) by a 20 N force directed 30° above the horizontal. Starting from rest: (a) find the normal force and friction force [3]; (b) find the acceleration [2]; (c) find the speed and distance after 4.0 s [2]; (d) verify the work-energy theorem over those 4 s by computing the work of the pull, the work of friction and the change in kinetic energy [3].\n\n**B2. (10 marks)** A 0.20 kg ball moving at +5.0 m/s collides head-on with a 0.30 kg ball moving at -2.0 m/s. (a) If the collision is perfectly elastic, find both final velocities and check the conservation laws [6]. (b) If it is perfectly inelastic, find the common velocity and the kinetic energy lost [4].\n\n**B3. (10 marks)** A 1.0 kg mass hangs from a light string wrapped around a uniform solid disc of mass 4.0 kg and radius 0.50 m, free to rotate about its axis. The mass is released from rest. (a) Find the acceleration of the mass and the string tension [5]. (b) Find the angular acceleration of the disc [2]. (c) Find the speed of the mass after falling 2.0 m, using energy conservation as a check [3].\n\n**B4. (10 marks)** (a) A 0.50 kg mass on a spring with k = 50 N/m oscillates with amplitude 0.10 m. Find the angular frequency, period, maximum speed, total energy, and the kinetic and potential energies at x = 0.060 m [6]. (b) A 1.2 m string fixed at both ends has a fundamental frequency of 100 Hz. Find the wave speed, and the tension if μ = 0.0050 kg/m [4].\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **B** · 2 **B** · 3 **B** · 4 **B** · 5 **C** · 6 **C** · 7 **C** · 8 **C** · 9 **A** · 10 **B** · 11 **B** · 12 **C** · 13 **C** · 14 **B** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **B** · 18 **A** · 19 **C** · 20 **C**\n\nBrief reasons:\n1. t = v/g = 2.04 s.\n2. d = (1/2)(20)(5) = 50 m.\n3. R ∝ sin 2θ is a maximum at 45°.\n4. a = F/m = 5 m/s^2.\n5. W = m g = 49 N.\n6. a = g sin 30° = 4.9 m/s^2.\n7. W = m g h = 196 J.\n8. (1/2)(2)(9) = 9 J.\n9. (1/2)(100)(0.04) = 2 J.\n10. No external impulse.\n11. Velocities are exchanged.\n12. Definition for a hoop.\n13. α = τ/I = 6.\n14. L is conserved when Στ_ext = 0.\n15. T ∝ r^(3/2), so 2^(3/2) = 2.83.\n16. T ∝ sqrt(m).\n17. T ∝ sqrt(L).\n18. v = f λ = 50 m/s.\n19. L = λ/2.\n20. β = 10 log(10) = 10 dB.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.** Take m = 5.0 kg, F = 20 N, θ = 30°.\n(a) Vertical balance: N + F sin θ = m g, so N = 49 - 10 = **39.0 N**. Friction f_k = μ_k N = 0.20 · 39.0 = **7.8 N**. (1 mark diagram or equations, 1 each value.)\n(b) Horizontal: F cos θ - f_k = m a, so a = (17.32 - 7.8)/5 = **1.90 m/s^2**.\n(c) v = a t = 1.904 · 4 = **7.62 m/s**. d = (1/2) a t^2 = 0.5 · 1.904 · 16 = **15.2 m**.\n(d) W_pull = F cos θ · d = 17.32 · 15.23 = **263.8 J**. W_friction = -7.8 · 15.23 = **-118.8 J**. (Normal force, weight: zero work.) Net W = 145.0 J. ΔK = (1/2)(5)(7.616)^2 = **145.0 J** ✓.\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Step 1: m1 = 0.20, v1 = 5.0; m2 = 0.30, v2 = -2.0.\nStep 2: Total momentum p = 1.0 - 0.6 = 0.4 kg m/s.\nStep 3: Elastic: v1' = [(m1 - m2) v1 + 2 m2 v2]/(m1 + m2) = [(-0.1)(5) + 2(0.3)(-2)]/0.5 = (-0.5 - 1.2)/0.5 = **-3.4 m/s**. v2' = [2 m1 v1 + (m2 - m1) v2]/(m1 + m2) = [2(0.2)(5) + (0.1)(-2)]/0.5 = (2 - 0.2)/0.5 = **+3.6 m/s**.\nStep 4: Checks: momentum 0.2(-3.4) + 0.3(3.6) = -0.68 + 1.08 = 0.4 ✓; kinetic energy before: 2.5 + 0.6 = 3.1 J; after: 1.156 + 1.944 = 3.1 J ✓; relative speed before 7.0, after 3.6 - (-3.4) = 7.0 ✓.\n(b) Step 1: v' = p/(m1 + m2) = 0.4/0.5 = **0.80 m/s**.\nStep 2: K after = (1/2)(0.5)(0.64) = 0.16 J.\nStep 3: **Energy lost = 3.1 - 0.16 = 2.94 J** (95%).\n\n**B3.** Let M = 4.0 kg, R = 0.50 m, I = (1/2) M R^2 = 0.50 kg m^2, m = 1.0 kg.\n(a) Step 1: For the mass: m g - T = m a.\nStep 2: For the disc: T R = I α = I a/R, so T = (1/2) M a.\nStep 3: Then m g = (m + M/2) a, so **a = 9.8/3 = 3.27 m/s^2**, and T = (1/2)(4)(3.267) = **6.53 N**.\nStep 4: Check: m(g - a) = 6.53 ✓.\n(b) α = a/R = **6.53 rad/s^2**.\n(c) v = sqrt(2 a d) = sqrt(2 · 3.267 · 2) = **3.61 m/s**. Energy check: m g h = (1/2) m v^2 + (1/2) I ω^2 = (1/2)(1)v^2 + (1/4)(4) v^2 = 1.5 v^2, so v^2 = 19.6/1.5 = 13.07 and v = 3.61 m/s ✓.\n\n**B4.**\n(a) ω = sqrt(k/m) = sqrt(100) = **10 rad/s**; T = 2π/ω = **0.628 s**; v_max = A ω = **1.0 m/s**; E = (1/2) k A^2 = (1/2)(50)(0.01) = **0.25 J**. At x = 0.060: U = (1/2)(50)(0.0036) = **0.090 J**; K = E - U = **0.160 J** (so v = 0.80 m/s).\n(b) f1 = v/(2L), so v = 2 L f1 = 2(1.2)(100) = **240 m/s**. v = sqrt(T/μ), so T = μ v^2 = 0.0050 · 57,600 = **288 N**.\n"},mcq:[{"q":"A ball is thrown straight up at 20 m/s. The time to reach the highest point is about","options":["1.0 s","2.0 s","4.0 s","20 s"],"answer":1,"explain":"t = v/g = 2.04 s.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A car accelerates uniformly from rest to 20 m/s in 5 s. The distance travelled is","options":["20 m","50 m","100 m","200 m"],"answer":1,"explain":"d = (1/2)(20)(5) = 50 m.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"On level ground and neglecting air resistance, the launch angle giving maximum range is","options":["30°","45°","60°","90°"],"answer":1,"explain":"R ∝ sin 2θ is a maximum at 45°.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A net force of 10 N acts on a 2 kg object. Its acceleration is","options":["0.2 m/s^2","5 m/s^2","12 m/s^2","20 m/s^2"],"answer":1,"explain":"a = F/m = 5 m/s^2.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The weight of a 5 kg object on Earth is about","options":["5 N","9.8 N","49 N","0.51 N"],"answer":2,"explain":"W = m g = 49 N.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A block slides down a frictionless 30° incline. Its acceleration is","options":["9.8 m/s^2","8.5 m/s^2","4.9 m/s^2","0"],"answer":2,"explain":"a = g sin 30° = 4.9 m/s^2.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The work done lifting a 10 kg mass 2 m at constant speed is about","options":["20 J","98 J","196 J","2000 J"],"answer":2,"explain":"W = m g h = 196 J.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The kinetic energy of a 2 kg object moving at 3 m/s is","options":["3 J","6 J","9 J","18 J"],"answer":2,"explain":"(1/2)(2)(9) = 9 J.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A spring with k = 100 N/m is stretched 0.20 m. The stored energy is","options":["2 J","4 J","10 J","20 J"],"answer":0,"explain":"(1/2)(100)(0.04) = 2 J.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Total momentum is conserved","options":["only in elastic collisions","in all collisions when external forces are negligible","only in inelastic collisions","never in collisions"],"answer":1,"explain":"No external impulse.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A moving ball makes a head-on elastic collision with an identical stationary ball. Afterwards","options":["both move at half speed","the first stops and the second moves with the original speed","both stop","the first rebounds"],"answer":1,"explain":"Velocities are exchanged.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The moment of inertia of a thin hoop of mass M and radius R about its axis is","options":["(1/2) M R^2","(2/5) M R^2","M R^2","(1/12) M R^2"],"answer":2,"explain":"Definition for a hoop.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A torque of 3 N m acts on a body with I = 0.5 kg m^2. The angular acceleration is","options":["1.5 rad/s^2","3.5 rad/s^2","6 rad/s^2","0.17 rad/s^2"],"answer":2,"explain":"α = τ/I = 6.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Angular momentum of a system is conserved when","options":["kinetic energy is constant","the net external torque is zero","the net external force is zero only","the speed is constant"],"answer":1,"explain":"L is conserved when Στ_ext = 0.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"If a satellite's orbital radius doubles, its period increases by a factor of","options":["2","2.83","4","8"],"answer":1,"explain":"T ∝ r^(3/2), so 2^(3/2) = 2.83.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"If the mass on a spring is quadrupled, the period","options":["quadruples","doubles","halves","is unchanged"],"answer":1,"explain":"T ∝ sqrt(m).","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"If the length of a simple pendulum is quadrupled, the period","options":["quadruples","doubles","halves","is unchanged"],"answer":1,"explain":"T ∝ sqrt(L).","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A wave has frequency 100 Hz and wavelength 0.5 m. Its speed is","options":["50 m/s","200 m/s","0.005 m/s","100.5 m/s"],"answer":0,"explain":"v = f λ = 50 m/s.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The fundamental standing wave on a string fixed at both ends of length L has wavelength","options":["L/2","L","2L","4L"],"answer":2,"explain":"L = λ/2.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Increasing sound intensity by a factor of 10 raises the intensity level by","options":["1 dB","3 dB","10 dB","100 dB"],"answer":2,"explain":"β = 10 log(10) = 10 dB.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A particle's position is x = 3t^2 - 2t (SI). Its velocity at t = 2 s is","options":["4 m/s","8 m/s","10 m/s","12 m/s"],"answer":2,"explain":"v = 6t - 2 = 10.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"An object falls from rest for 4.0 s (no air resistance). The distance fallen is about","options":["39 m","78 m","157 m","39.2 m/s"],"answer":1,"explain":"(1/2)(9.8)(16) = 78.4 m.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"At the highest point of a projectile's path (no air resistance), the velocity is","options":["zero","purely horizontal","purely vertical","at 45°"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only the vertical component vanishes.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A car moves at 10 m/s around a curve of radius 5 m. Its centripetal acceleration is","options":["2 m/s^2","10 m/s^2","20 m/s^2","50 m/s^2"],"answer":2,"explain":"v^2/r = 20.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A 10 kg block on a floor (μ_k = 0.30) is pushed at constant speed. The horizontal force needed is","options":["3 N","29.4 N","98 N","0"],"answer":1,"explain":"f_k = 0.3(98) = 29.4 N.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The work done by gravity on a 2 kg object that falls 5 m is","options":["-98 J","10 J","98 J","196 J"],"answer":2,"explain":"m g h = 98 J (positive work, since the force and displacement are both downward).","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A machine does 100 J of work in 4 s. Its power is","options":["4 W","25 W","100 W","400 W"],"answer":1,"explain":"P = W/t.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A 4 kg cart moving at 4 m/s collides and sticks to an identical stationary cart. The final speed is","options":["1 m/s","2 m/s","4 m/s","8 m/s"],"answer":1,"explain":"Momentum: 16 = 8v, so v = 2.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A 10 N force acts for 3.0 s on a 2.0 kg object initially at rest. Its final speed is","options":["5 m/s","6 m/s","15 m/s","30 m/s"],"answer":2,"explain":"J = 30 N s, v = 15 m/s.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The moment of inertia of a uniform rod (mass M, length L) about one end is","options":["M L^2/12","M L^2/3","M L^2/2","M L^2"],"answer":1,"explain":"M L^2/3.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For a solid sphere rolling without slipping, the fraction of the kinetic energy that is rotational is","options":["2/5","2/7","1/2","5/7"],"answer":1,"explain":"(2/5)/(1 + 2/5) = 2/7.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The ratio of escape speed to circular orbital speed at the same radius is","options":["1","sqrt 2","2","1/sqrt 2"],"answer":1,"explain":"sqrt(2 G M/r)/sqrt(G M/r).","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For circular orbits around the same planet, orbital speed varies with radius r as","options":["r","1/r","1/sqrt r","r^2"],"answer":2,"explain":"v = sqrt(G M/r).","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For SHM, at maximum displacement the","options":["speed is maximum","acceleration is zero","speed is zero and acceleration is maximum","kinetic energy is maximum"],"answer":2,"explain":"At x = A, v = 0 and a = ω^2 A.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A damped oscillator is critically damped when","options":["b = 0","γ = ω0","γ > ω0","γ < ω0"],"answer":1,"explain":"Critical damping.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A driven lightly damped oscillator has maximum amplitude when the drive frequency is","options":["very small","close to the natural frequency","very large","twice the natural frequency"],"answer":1,"explain":"Resonance.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"If the tension in a string is quadrupled, the wave speed","options":["quadruples","doubles","halves","is unchanged"],"answer":1,"explain":"v ∝ sqrt(T).","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A pipe closed at one end supports","options":["all harmonics","only odd harmonics","only even harmonics","no standing waves"],"answer":1,"explain":"Node at the closed end, antinode at the open end.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A source of sound approaches a stationary listener. The observed frequency is","options":["lower than the source frequency","the same","higher","zero"],"answer":2,"explain":"Doppler shift upward.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A sound level of 40 dB corresponds to an intensity of","options":["10^-4 W/m^2","10^-8 W/m^2","10^-12 W/m^2","40 W/m^2"],"answer":1,"explain":"β = 10 log(I/10^-12) = 40, so I = 10^-8.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
